WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.710
250 mph— that's the speed of the
fastest cars in the world. It's also

2
00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.460
the speed at which messages travel from
our brain to the rest of the body,

3
00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:15.360
telling our legs to walk or our hand to
grab a drink when we're thirsty. That's

4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.580
incredibly fast and it's necessary in
order to allow us to interact with and

5
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.980
respond to the world around us. These
messages travel in the form of

6
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:30.560
electricity down specific paths made up
of long chains of cells called neurons.

7
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.160
The neuron is a very unusual type of
cell in the body because it has very

8
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.820
long extensions. The neuron has a cell
body, which contains a nucleus,

9
00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:44.470
surrounded by cell membrane extensions,
or processes called dendrites, which

10
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.450
act like antennae to receive signals
from other neurons. The neuron then

11
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:54.990
extends a single, very long process
called an axon that ends in the axon

12
00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:59.670
terminal. Some axons can be very long,
one meter in the case of some neurons

13
00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.950
in your leg. The electrical signal
travels from the cell body, down the

14
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.350
axon, to the terminal. The terminal is
then triggered to release a chemical

15
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:13.750
signal that travels from that neuron to
another one. This starts an electrical

16
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:18.940
current in that second neuron. And so
electrical signals pass from one neuron

17
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.780
to another to another, allowing a
thought that starts in your brain to

18
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.460
travel to a muscle in your foot.

19
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:31.000
Since axons are the long distance wires
in this communication circuit, fast

20
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.320
transmission of the electrical signal
down the axon is crucial for us to

21
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:41.500
chase down a fly away umbrella or jump
out of the way of a falling rock. To

22
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:46.610
enable this rapid transport of a
message along the axon, our bodies rely

23
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:46.970
on myelin.

24
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.900
Although observed as part of the
nervous system as early as 1717, myelin

25
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:59.370
was first named as such by Rudolf
Virchow, a German physician, writer and

26
00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:05.920
politician, in 1854. Scientists
theorized a few years later that myelin

27
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.530
was providing the axon with insulation,
which as in a wire, allowed the

28
00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:16.930
electrical signal to move fast with
minimal signal loss as it traveled down

29
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.950
the neuron. This idea proved to be
correct.

30
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.590
Now we know much more about myelin. It
is a fatty substance that covers the

31
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.500
axon in long sections. The fatty myelin
is actually long extensions of cell

32
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.920
membranes made by supporting cells
called glia that wrap around the axons

33
00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:41.730
in concentric circles. In the central
nervous system, meaning the brain and

34
00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:46.830
spinal cord, the glial cells that wrap
their membranes around axons are called

35
00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:47.230
oligodendrocytes.

36
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.110
And in the peripheral nervous system,
in the nerves that travel throughout

37
00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.650
the body, they are called Schwann cells.

38
00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:04.910
The myelin wrapping is separated by
very short, unmyelinated gaps that were

39
00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:10.140
spotted by French physician,
Louis-Antoine Ranvier. They were then

40
00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.450
named after him as the nodes of
Ranvier. The electrical signal jumps

41
00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:19.200
between these nodes, traveling down the
neuron. Without the nodes, transport

42
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.860
would be at least 15 times slower, not
enough for us vertebrate humans.

43
00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.590
However, some animals, such as the
squid and octopus have developed

44
00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:34.260
creative ways to enable fast message
transport without using myelin. These

45
00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.230
creatures have axons with a much larger
diameter, which reduces the resistance

46
00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:44.270
the signal experiences in a thinner
axon, allowing the signal to still move

47
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:49.260
quickly through the animal's body and
allow them to escape from predators.

48
00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.090
In humans, if the nerve cells become
demyelinated, the transmission of the

49
00:03:55.090 --> 00:04:02.880
electrical message can be slowed from
the normal 250 mph to 100 mph. Multiple

50
00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.940
sclerosis, or MS, is a disease caused
by loss of myelin. The oligodendrocytes

51
00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:14.180
that form the myelin are lost. In MS,
message transport and delivery are

52
00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:19.740
slower in the spinal cord, brain and
eyes. Patients develop symptoms such as

53
00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.550
fatigue and trouble with vision, and in
the long run, can suffer brain damage.

54
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.190
While there is no cure yet for multiple
sclerosis, many scientists around the

55
00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:34.910
world are working to try and understand
the underlying cause of MS and other

56
00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:40.460
disorders involving myelin. Treatments
that preserve or replace the myelin

57
00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.330
producing cells could transform the
lives of the millions of people

58
00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:47.860
currently living with MS in the United
States.

